## Annotations in Contract Test Code

When we create tests using nunit-console like

```bash
nunit-console.exe ArrayListTest.dll 
	/framework:4.0 
	/nodereuse:on 
	/explicit 
	/include:ArrayList 
	/xml:output.xml
	/domain:Dynamic 
	/profiler:off 
```

In this test suite we include `ArrayList`, which means that all test methods with names that start with `PUT_` are included. Each method is run under different assumptions about what the behavior might be, so the coverage is pretty diverse.

**POSTCONDITION**: Always the last line of the method body is annotated as follows:

```csharp
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
```

This line annotates the value "New_arrListLastIndexOfX" as a value that will be shown in a user interface for examining the results of the test.

In the actual test result, we can see:

```caml
<Variable name='New_arrListLastIndexOf_x' isLast='1'><Value>-1</Value><Count>1</Count><Unique>-1</Unique></Variable>
```

This indicates that `New_arrListLastIndexOf_x == -1`, so a value was not found in the list anymore, which is correct as we're modifying the list and removing `x` which we inserted earlier.

**PRECONDITION**: Before the method body starts, we annotate the initial state of the array list and the variables it manipulates.

```csharp
int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
int Old_x = x;
int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);
```

Then we perform the operation on the array list.

```csharp
arrList[index] = x;
```

After that, we annotate the changes.

```csharp
New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);
```

And show all variables using `PexObserve`.

We would like to point out that Pex doesn't really execute the test. It generates many different test cases then runs the actual method under each case, so it's important that the method doesn't have any side effect. In an ideal scenario, mutating the parameters of the method would be avoided or made part of return values.

## Instrumented Unit Tests for Non-Static Methods

If a non-static method under test mutates its instance members then all tests should:

- have a single method parameter that represents the instance being tested.

For example:

Instead of
	
```csharp
[Test]
[Explicit]
public void ArrayList_SetIndexer_ItemAssigned()
{
  ArrayList list = new ArrayList { 1, 2, 3 };
  list[2] = "Bananas";
  Assert.AreEqual(1, list[2]);
}
```

Do this:

```csharp
[Test]
[Explicit]
[Arguments(1, 2, "Bananas")]
public void ArrayList_SetIndexer_ItemAssigned(int oldValue, int index, string value)
{
    this.Arrange().ArrayList[index] = oldValue;
    this.Act().ArrayList[index] = value;
    this.Assert().AreEqual(value, this.ArrayList[index]);
}
```

## Instrumented Unit Tests for Static Methods

If a static method under test mutates its class members then all tests should:

- have at least one non-'params' argument, that represents instance under test.

For example:

Instead of

```csharp
[Test]
[Explicit]
public void ArrayList_AddObject_ItemAddedToEnd()
{    
    ArrayList list--------------------	    //Debug.Assert((New_arrListLastIndexOfX != Old_arrListCount));
        }
    }

}


