I am trying to create Unit Test for ArrayList in Code Contract Style. But I keep running into errors, even though I have defined assumptions and preconditions, I don't know how to fix these errors. 
This is the error message I keep getting:

An exception of type 'Pex.Framework.Exceptions.PexRethrownException' occurred in Pex.Framework.Exceptions.dll but was not handled in user code

Additional information:

An internal exception 'Pex.Framework.Exceptions.PexSettingsException' occurred in Pex.Framework.Exceptions.dll but was not handled in user code

Additional information:

System.Private.CoreLib: Exception while executing the method 'System_Boolean System_Collections_ArrayList_Exists_System_Predicate_ object_)' in  'System.Private.CoreLib, Version=4.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=7cec85c7bea7798e'. The method might have been called via reflection or a different mechanism.
    at PexAssemblyReflectionImplementation.ExecuteMethod(Exception exceptionToRethrow)
    at PexExecutionManager.ExecuteMethod(MethodInvocation methodInvocation, Boolean throwIfThrowsReflectionException)

The error is specific to the unit tests I'm writing for ArrayList in Code Contract style. I'm trying to follow the examples given by Pex. Pex gives you the ability to create unit test contracts that can run to verify the correctness of your code contracts.
Any suggestions on how to fix these issues? 

A: Unfortunately, PEX (the tool that is typically used for testing code contracts, especially those for collections like ArrayList) has issues executing tests on types like ArrayList. It may not be capable to run tests of PUT methods (which are meant for testing if the ArrayList works correctly). 
While it’s still possible to perform exploration testing on a single method that doesn’t depend on external state (pure functions), PEX might not be successful testing the mutator methods for ArrayList when you have a non-readonly property (like Count) which could change if the method does anything (like mutates the object).
The error you're experiencing generally happened because of PEX limitations testing certain types of methods. It's suggested that this tool is more reliable when unit testing methods that don't depend on external state such as pure functions.
In this case, you might have to use other tools like NUnit for testing code contracts like this. 

Also another point to note is--------------------
	     // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is not same as old array size 

	     // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is not same as old array size 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is not same as old array size 

	     // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x in new array is not same as old array size 

        }
    }
}

